00:00:00.730 --> 00:00:04.930
Leah: To understand what coding is all
about and how it works, you first have to

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:07.390
know how a computer system processes data.

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:10.770
To do this we will start by
looking at a central processing

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:13.449
unit or CPU and what it does.

00:00:14.009 --> 00:00:17.439
Jack: CPUs are silicon chips
found in almost all devices from

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:21.120
smartwatches to computers and
act as the brain of the device.

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:26.690
The CPU also called the main processor is
the part of the device that is responsible

00:00:26.690 --> 00:00:28.990
for processing and executing instructions.

00:00:29.599 --> 00:00:34.270
The CPU is the core component that
defines a computer but can only

00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:36.070
function alongside other hardware.

00:00:36.719 --> 00:00:40.019
This silicon chip sits in a
socket on a main circuit board or

00:00:40.019 --> 00:00:41.670
mother board inside the device.

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:45.960
It is connected to all other aspects
of the hardware, and integrates

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:49.360
the various signals and processes
to produce the overall function.

00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:55.580
Leah: CPUs consist of billions of
microscopic transistors which allow it to

00:00:55.580 --> 00:01:00.010
make calculations needed to run programs
that are stored on a system's memory.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:05.509
These transistors are like little
switches that turn on and off conveying

00:01:05.519 --> 00:01:10.140
the '1's and '0's that translate into
everything that is done on a computer

00:01:10.469 --> 00:01:12.810
from watching videos to writing an email.

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:19.170
This system of ‘1’s and ‘0’s is referred
to as binary, meaning that there are two

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:22.680
possible states: on [‘1’] and off [‘0’].

00:01:24.289 --> 00:01:28.929
Circuits consisting of binary inputs
controlled by transistors, form the

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:33.600
basis of all commercially available
contemporary computational devices.

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:37.320
Jack: CPUs take instructions
from a program or application

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:38.830
and perform a calculation.

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:43.820
This process is in 3 stages, it
'fetches' the instruction from the

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:46.229
RAM, 'decodes' it and 'executes' it.

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:52.620
The executed instruction or calculation
can involve basic arithmetic, comparing

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:56.330
numbers, performing a function, or
moving numbers around in memory.

00:01:57.410 --> 00:02:03.239
Leah: In modern systems the CPU feeds data
to specialised hardware as it is required.

00:02:03.799 --> 00:02:07.920
The main component of CPU chips
are the processing units, which

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:09.380
are referred to as ‘cores’.

00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:14.599
Originally CPU's had single
processing cores but modern ones

00:02:14.599 --> 00:02:18.090
have multiple cores allowing multiple
instructions to be given at once.

00:02:19.490 --> 00:02:24.110
Jack: Most modern CPUs have 2 or
4 cores, 6 are mainstream and more

00:02:24.190 --> 00:02:27.100
expensive chips can have from 8 up to 64.

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:32.160
Clock speed measured in gigahertz,
denotes how many instructions a CPU

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:36.320
can handle per second and a faster
clock speed means a faster processor.

